From: OpenWrt HA build <local>
Subject: [PATCH] owsync: one session at a time, a failed session tried again, shorter timeouts

A daemon started a session to its peers once, when it started and when a
scan found a change. If that session failed, because the peer was being
restarted, its link was down or its owsync was not listening yet, nothing
tried again: the change stayed on one node until the next change or the
next restart. Seen on a router whose owsync started two seconds after its
sync interface came up.

The poller now keeps, for every peer, whether a session to it is owed:
from the start of the daemon, from every change a scan finds, and from
every session with it that did not end well. Only a session this node
started and that ended well clears it. Until then the session is tried
again, first after one to two seconds, then after up to twice as long
each time, up to the poll interval. Every wait is drawn anew and kept to
the millisecond: two nodes that turned each other away at the same moment
would, with equal waits or waits on the ticks of a clock, come back at
the same moment for ever. The first failure is logged as an error, the
attempts after it at debug level, and the success with the number of
attempts it took.

Sessions no longer run side by side. Two at once worked on the same files
and the same database with nothing between them: both nodes start a
session to each other whenever both start together or both find a change
in the same poll, and the poller scanned while a session a peer had
started was writing. One lock, in the process and (a lock file next to
the database) between a daemon and a one-shot command, is held by a
session and by the poller's scan. A session that finds it taken does not
wait, which would leave two nodes waiting for each other: a connection
from a peer is closed at once and the peer tries again, and a session of
this node's own returns OWSYNC_ERROR_BUSY and is tried again shortly.
Whatever leaves a peer without a session it was owed - a session it
started that ended badly or was turned away, a change that a session's
scan found before the poller did - makes the daemon sync to its peers
again.

With one session at a time a peer that stalls keeps every other session
out, so the timeout of a read or write on a session's socket is 30
seconds instead of 300, and a connection to a peer is given 10 seconds
instead of the kernel's two minutes. A source address that cannot be
bound (its interface is down) is no longer skipped: the connection would
leave through whatever route there is, with a source the peer does not
accept.

The wire protocol does not change. A node without the patch whose session
is turned away does not try again; the node with the patch does, and
brings over what both hold.

